hexagon: htp: remove only the mappings that a batch needs to free
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The defect

The DSP maps each buffer of a batch into its VA (prep_op_bufs of htp/main.c).
When the new mappings do not fit in max_vmem, it removed each mapping that the
batch does not use. The 4B Q8_0 has 4275 MiB of weights in chunks of a maximum
of 1 GiB (GGML_HEXAGON_MBUF), and max_vmem is 3158 MiB. A decode token runs in
4 batches, and each batch removed and mapped 1 to 2.4 GiB. On the phone the
prologues of the 4 batches took 220, 250, 283 and 538 us, 1.29 ms of each
token. A batch of the 2B, which fits, has a prologue of 20 us. On the phone one
HAP_mmap2 of 1 GiB takes about 17 us and one HAP_munmap2 about 35 us (tools/ddrbw
mapcost), thus the calls explain only about 0.4 ms of the 1.29 ms. Each map and
each removal also wrote a FARF(ALWAYS) line.

The change

The request of a batch gets a flags field (htp_opbatch_req, 32 bytes). With the
flag HTP_OPBATCH_MMAP_LRU (the switch GGML_HEXAGON_MMAP_LRU, preset 1),
evict_mmaps removes one mapping at a time, and it stops when the new mappings
fit in max_vmem and in the table. It removes a mapping with the lowest batch
number of its last use (htp_mmap.last_use). Among such mappings it removes the
smallest one that frees sufficient bytes, else the largest one. The value 0 of
the switch keeps the previous removal.

The table has 32 slots and not 16, because more mappings stay. The mask of the
table uses 1u << i, because 1 << 31 is an overflow of an int. The map and unmap
lines are FARF(HIGH).

The tests

The fuzz target fuzz_mmap of tests/fuzz/hexhost copies the functions of the
table from htp/main.c and runs them against a fake VA: a first-fit allocator
with a capacity, thus a map can fail and the second pass runs. After each batch
it compares the map and unmap calls and the table with a model of the policy of
the flag. It also makes sure that each buffer of a batch has a mapping at its
base, that the table holds a maximum of max_vmem bytes after the first pass,
and that a removal before a second pass never takes a mapping of the batch. It
ran 60 s in the configurations none, asan, ubsan, tsan and msan, each with the
debug and the release profile, and it found nothing.

An earlier host test ran the table of the series before this patch against the
same fake VA. With the flag 0, the map and unmap events are the events of the
previous code. On 8 tokens of the cycle of the 4 decode batches of the 4B, the
policy does 44 maps and 38 removals, against 58 maps and 56 removals. With the
costs of the calls above, the policy costs less on 32 of 121 random cycles,
more on 4 (a maximum of 17 %), and the same on 85. Thus the policy is not better
for each sequence, and the switch stays.

The phone

The stage quick ran the 4B Q8_0 in the app configuration. It measured this
patch together with the next two patches and with a change of the rows of the
pointwise unary ops, which is not in the series:

- Against HEAD, tg32 at the depth 0 is +1.50 % and pp512 is +3.38 %. The next
  phone batch measures the three patches without the row change.
- The sum of the batch prologues of each decode token is 1265 us with HEAD,
  1075 us with GGML_HEXAGON_MMAP_LRU=0 and 916 us with the preset value
  (medians of 7 tokens, from the profile of the DSP).
- The logits hashes of a prompt of 1024 tokens and 16 decode tokens are the
  same with GGML_HEXAGON_MMAP_LRU=0 and with the preset value.

